            <div xml:id="d849">

                <pb facs="0700" n="692" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg692"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.5034 (China) Eastern Publishing Co./30:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-12-30T09:00:00+08:00">December 30, 1937—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 30—7 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1248. Reference my despatch No. 956 of September 11, 1937, and previous
                    communications regarding “<hi rendition="#italic">Voice of China</hi>” published
                    by Eastern Publishing Company, owned by Max Granich. Settlement police report
                    that Mr. and Mrs. Max Granich left Shanghai on December 21, 1937, on the
                    steamship <hi rendition="#italic">Ranpura</hi> destined for Marseille,
                    France.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
